INT. HOSPITAL MRI SUITE - NIGHT

A hum like a held note. The scanner sits dark. A patient's gurney is parked beside it, empty. Rain taps a high window.

DR. THEODORE ABIODUN, 50s, rubs his eyes at the control desk. BERNADETTE KOWALSKI, 70s, in a hospital gown and slippers, sits on the edge of a plastic chair. He notices her only when he turns.

He rises. She does not.

THEODORE
Mrs. Kowalski. You're supposed to be upstairs.

BERNADETTE
I couldn't sleep.

THEODORE
You could have rung for a nurse.

BERNADETTE
They told me the scan was clean.

THEODORE
It is.

BERNADETTE
Then why are you here at one in the morning?

THEODORE
I like the quiet.

BERNADETTE
Doctors don't like quiet. They like numbers.

He pulls a stool close and sits. Neither speaks for a moment.

THEODORE
Your daughter called.

BERNADETTE
She calls every day.

THEODORE
She asked me to check you were comfortable.

BERNADETTE
She asked you to lie to me.

THEODORE
Mrs. Kowalski.

BERNADETTE
Dr. Abiodun. Do I look like a woman who needs to be handled?

He considers this. He lifts a folder from the desk and sets it in her lap.

THEODORE
Fourteen months. Perhaps sixteen. I should have said so this afternoon.

BERNADETTE
You were kind enough not to.

THEODORE
I was a coward.

BERNADETTE
You were both. It's allowed.

She opens the folder, glances at the images, and closes it. Her hands rest on top of it, perfectly still.

BERNADETTE (CONT'D)
My husband went in eleven weeks. Fourteen months is a luxury.

THEODORE
I'm sorry.

BERNADETTE
Don't be. Sit with me a while.

THEODORE
I will.

BERNADETTE
Tell me about the quiet.

THEODORE
It's mostly the machine.

BERNADETTE
Then listen to it with me.

The hum swells softly. They sit side by side, two figures in the blue half-light, as the rain eases.
